      "total_response_seconds": 9.3751,
      "response": "\u201cThe Arab Tent\u201d is an oil\u2011on\u2011canvas painting by Edwin (Henry)\u202fLandseer, dated around\u202f1865\u201366.  It belongs to the English school and is catalogued in our collection as inventory\u202fP376, displayed in the Hall.\n\nThe work shows an Arab mare and her foal beneath a tent that, despite its original exhibition title \u201cMare and Foal \u2013 Indian Tent\u2026\u201d, is clearly an Arab tent.  Around them are two Persian greyhounds and two monkeys\u2014one with an earring\u2014arranged so that the animals become increasingly \u201cintelligent\u201d toward the top of the canvas.  A jar with two pipes on the right hints at the owner\u2019s presence.\n\nFirst shown at the Royal Academy in 1866, the painting was bought by the Prince of Wales from Landseer in 1867 and later acquired by Sir\u202fRichard\u202fWallace in 1878 for \u00a36,300, one of the most expensive purchases by a Wallace founder.  Its recent conservation in 2017 was funded by Henrietta, Duchess of Bedford.",
